---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ipsas/ipsas3.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  covers={metadata.covers}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Objective, Scope & Definitions" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Selection & Application of Accounting Policies" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Consistency & Changes in Accounting Policies" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Applying Changes – Retrospective Application" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Disclosure of Changes in Accounting Policy" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Changes in Accounting Estimates" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Errors – Definition, Correction & Disclosure" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Impracticability Framework" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Effective Date & Amendment Timeline" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Relationship to IAS 8" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Worked Examples" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement / Process Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="active"
    explanation="IPSAS 3 Accounting Policies, Changes in Accounting Estimates and Errors is the current, active standard issued by the IPSASB. It is effective for annual financial statements covering periods beginning on/after 1 January 2008 (earlier application encouraged). It superseded the 2000 IPSAS 3; the current Standard comprises paras. 1–61, all of equal authority (paras. 5–6 deleted)."
    link=""
    linkText=""
  />

      <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Objective, Scope &amp; Definitions (paras. 1–7)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Objective &amp; scope</div>
        <div class="principle">Prescribes criteria for selecting and changing accounting policies, plus the accounting treatment and disclosure of (a) changes in accounting policies, (b) changes in accounting estimates, and (c) corrections of errors – to enhance relevance, faithful representation, and comparability over time and across entities.</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Applied in selecting/applying accounting policies, and accounting for policy changes, estimate changes, and prior period error corrections</span>
        </div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Tax effects of error corrections and retrospective adjustments are OUT of scope – not relevant for many public sector entities</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Disclosure requirements for accounting policies generally (other than CHANGES) sit in IPSAS 1, not here. Paras. 5–6 [Deleted].</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Accounting policies</div><div class="glossary-def">The specific principles, bases, conventions, rules, and practices applied in preparing and presenting financial statements.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Change in accounting estimate</div><div class="glossary-def">An adjustment to the carrying amount of an asset/liability, or periodic consumption of an asset, from assessing its present status and expected future benefits/obligations. Results from NEW information – not error correction.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Prior period errors</div><div class="glossary-def">Omissions/misstatements from failing to use, or misusing, faithfully representative information that was available and could reasonably have been obtained when prior statements were authorized for issue.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Retrospective application / restatement</div><div class="glossary-def">Applying a new policy, or correcting an error, as if it had always been applied / the error had never occurred.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Selection &amp; Application of Accounting Policies (paras. 9–15)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Step 1 – an IPSAS specifically applies</div>
        <div class="principle">Where an IPSAS specifically applies to a transaction, other event, or condition, the policy applied SHALL be determined by applying that Standard. Immaterial departures are never acceptable, even to achieve a particular presentation.</div>
        <div class="note">Guidance stated as an INTEGRAL PART of an IPSAS is mandatory; guidance that is not does not itself impose requirements.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Step 2 – no IPSAS specifically applies: the hierarchy (para. 14–15)</div>
        <div class="principle">Management uses judgment to develop a policy that is relevant to accountability/decision-making needs, faithfully represents financial position/performance/cash flows, and meets understandability, timeliness, comparability, and verifiability.</div>
        <div class="elabel">SOURCES, IN ORDER (PARA. 14)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>(a) Requirements in IPSAS dealing with similar/related issues</span>
          <span>(b) Definitions, recognition &amp; measurement criteria in the Conceptual Framework</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">MAY ALSO CONSIDER (PARA. 15) – ONLY IF CONSISTENT WITH THE ABOVE</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Most recent pronouncements of other standard-setters (e.g. IASB/IFRS, IFRIC/SIC)</span>
          <span>Accepted public or private sector practices</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Consistency &amp; Changes in Accounting Policies (paras. 16–23)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Consistency requirement (para. 16)</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Policies are selected and applied CONSISTENTLY for similar transactions, events, and conditions – unless an IPSAS specifically requires/permits categorization for which different policies may apply, in which case a consistent policy per category is used.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">When a policy change is permitted (para. 17)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Required by an IPSAS; OR</span>
          <span>Results in faithfully representative and MORE RELEVANT information about the entity's position, performance, or cash flows</span>
        </div>
        <div class="elabel">NOT POLICY CHANGES (PARA. 21)</div>
        <div class="cross-list">
          <span>Applying a policy to transactions that differ in substance from previous ones</span>
          <span>Applying a NEW policy to transactions that didn't previously occur or were immaterial</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">Initial application of a policy to revalue assets under IPSAS 31/45 is a policy change, but is dealt with as a REVALUATION under those Standards, not under IPSAS 3 (para. 22–23).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Applying Changes in Accounting Policy – Retrospective Application (paras. 24–32)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Which approach applies</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Change from INITIAL APPLICATION of an IPSAS with transitional provisions → follow those specific provisions</span>
          <span>Change from initial application WITHOUT transitional provisions, or a VOLUNTARY change → apply RETROSPECTIVELY</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Early application of a new Standard is NOT a voluntary policy change (para. 25).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Retrospective application mechanics (para. 27)</div>
        <div class="principle">Adjust the opening balance of EACH affected net assets/equity component for the earliest period presented, and other comparative amounts, as if the new policy had always applied.</div>
        <div class="elabel">LIMITATIONS – WHEN IMPRACTICABLE (PARAS. 28–30)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Can't determine period-specific effects → apply prospectively from the earliest period practicable, adjusting that period's opening net assets/equity balance</span>
          <span>Can't determine the CUMULATIVE effect at all → apply the new policy PROSPECTIVELY from the earliest date practicable</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Disclosure of Changes in Accounting Policy (paras. 33–36)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Initial application of an IPSAS (para. 33)</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>Title of the Standard</span>
          <span>Whether the change follows transitional provisions, and a description of them</span>
          <span>Nature of the change; transitional provisions that might affect future periods</span>
          <span>Amount of adjustment per line item, current period &amp; each prior period presented (to the extent practicable)</span>
          <span>Amount relating to periods before those presented</span>
          <span>If impracticable for a particular period, the circumstances and how/from when the change was applied</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Voluntary changes (para. 34) &amp; impending standards (para. 35–36)</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Same disclosure set as above, PLUS the reasons why the new policy provides faithfully representative and more relevant information.</div>
        <div class="elabel">NOT YET APPLIED A NEW IPSAS (ISSUED BUT NOT EFFECTIVE)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Disclose that fact, plus known/reasonably estimable information on the expected impact</span>
          <span>Consider disclosing: title, nature of the change, required application date, planned initial-application date, and expected impact (or a statement that it isn't known/estimable)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Financial statements of SUBSEQUENT periods need not repeat these disclosures.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Changes in Accounting Estimates (paras. 37–45)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Why estimates are needed &amp; the policy/estimate boundary</div>
        <div class="elabel">TYPICAL ESTIMATES (PARA. 37)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Tax revenue due to government; bad debts on uncollected taxes</span>
          <span>Inventory obsolescence; fair value of financial assets/liabilities</span>
          <span>Useful lives / consumption pattern of depreciable assets; percentage completion; warranty obligations</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">A change in MEASUREMENT BASIS is a change in accounting POLICY, not an estimate. Where the two are hard to distinguish, treat it as a change in ESTIMATE (para. 40).</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Recognition – prospective only</div>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <div class="f-eq">Effect of an estimate change → recognized in surplus/deficit PROSPECTIVELY</div>
        </div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Period of the change, if it affects that period only (e.g. a bad-debt estimate revision)</span>
          <span>The period of change AND future periods, if both are affected (e.g. a revised useful life affecting future depreciation)</span>
          <span>If it changes assets/liabilities or a net assets/equity item directly, adjust the CARRYING AMOUNT in the period of change (para. 42)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Disclosure: nature and amount of the change, current-period effect plus expected future-period effect (unless impracticable to estimate – then disclose that fact) (paras. 44–45).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Errors – Definition, Correction &amp; Disclosure (paras. 46–54)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">What counts as an error</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Mathematical mistakes</span>
          <span>Mistakes in applying accounting policies</span>
          <span>Oversights or misinterpretations of facts</span>
          <span>Fraud</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger callout--flush">Financial statements do NOT comply with IPSAS if they contain material errors, OR immaterial errors made INTENTIONALLY to achieve a particular presentation.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Correction mechanics (para. 47) &amp; disclosure (para. 54)</div>
        <div class="principle">Material prior period errors are corrected RETROSPECTIVELY in the first financial statements authorized for issue after discovery – restating prior-period comparatives, or (if the error predates the earliest period presented) restating opening balances of assets, liabilities, and net assets/equity.</div>
        <div class="elabel">DISCLOSE (PARA. 54)</div>
        <div class="dot-list">
          <span>Nature of the prior period error</span>
          <span>Amount of the correction per line item, for each prior period presented (to the extent practicable)</span>
          <span>Amount of the correction at the beginning of the earliest period presented</span>
          <span>If retrospective restatement is impracticable for a period, the circumstances and how/from when the error was corrected</span>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Corrections are EXCLUDED from surplus/deficit for the period the error is discovered – they hit the comparatives instead. Distinguished from a change in estimate: e.g. resolving a contingency's actual outcome is not an error correction (para. 53).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Impracticability Framework (paras. 55–58)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2">
        <div class="ctitle cw">The impracticability test – same standard applies to policy changes AND error corrections</div>
        <div class="principle">A requirement is IMPRACTICABLE when the entity cannot apply it after making every reasonable effort. For retrospective application/restatement specifically, this arises when:</div>
        <div class="check-list">
          <span>The effects are not determinable</span>
          <span>It requires assumptions about what management's INTENT would have been in that period</span>
          <span>It requires significant estimates, and it's impossible to objectively distinguish information that (i) evidences circumstances existing at the relevant date(s), from (ii) information that would have been available when those prior statements were authorized for issue</span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout danger callout--flush"><strong>No hindsight allowed:</strong> when applying a new policy or correcting an error for a prior period, an entity does NOT use hindsight – neither in assuming what management's intentions would have been, nor in estimating amounts. Example: correcting a prior-period misclassification of a government building does not get revised for a LATER management decision to use it differently; correcting a pollution-cleanup liability estimate disregards a large spill discovered in a LATER period.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Effective Date &amp; Amendment Timeline (paras. 59–61)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="ctitle cn">Amendment history at a glance</div>
        <div class="principle">Base Standard: periods beginning on/after <strong>1 January 2008</strong> (earlier application encouraged). Supersedes IPSAS 3 (2000), <em>Net Surplus or Deficit for the Period, Fundamental Errors and Changes in Accounting Policies</em> (para. 61, "Withdrawal"). Appendix – Amendments to Other IPSAS – is [Deleted].</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>Effective</span><span>Change</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2011</span><span>Paras. 9, 11, 14 – Improvements to IPSAS (Jan 2010)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2017</span><span>Para. 60 (IPSAS 33); paras. 7, 10, 12, 14, 15, 17, 34 amended, para. 13 deleted (Improvements to IPSAS 2015)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2018</span><span>Paras. 5, 6 deleted – The Applicability of IPSAS (Apr 2016)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2025</span><span>Para. 22 – IPSAS 45, Property, Plant &amp; Equipment (May 2023)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2.2fr;"><span>1 Jan 2025</span><span>Para. 57 – IPSAS 46, Measurement (May 2023)</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">Para. 60: when an entity adopts accrual-basis IPSAS after the effective date (per IPSAS 33), this Standard applies from the date of adoption.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS – Comparison with IAS 8</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – IPSAS 3 vs IAS 8</div>
        <div class="principle">IPSAS 3 is drawn primarily from IAS 8 (2003), <em>Accounting Policies, Changes in Accounting Estimates and Errors</em>, including the IASB's May 2008 Improvements to IFRS.</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Topic</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IPSAS 3</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">IAS 8</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Terminology</span><span>"Statement of financial performance," "accumulated surplus/deficit," "net assets/equity"</span><span>"Income statement," "retained earnings," "equity"</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Income vs revenue</span><span>Does not use "income" – narrower "revenue" only</span><span>Uses the broader "income"</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Definitions (para. 7)</span><span>Distinct set of technical-term definitions</span><span>Different definition set</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>Policy hierarchy</span><span>Similar structure to IAS 8</span><span>Similar structure</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1.4fr 1.4fr;"><span>EPS adjustment disclosures</span><span>NOT required</span><span>Requires disclosure of the adjustment to basic/diluted EPS</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Examples (Implementation Guidance IG1–IG17)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Example 1 – retrospective restatement of a prior period error</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">CU6,500 of 20X1 tax revenue was omitted and wrongly recognized in 20X2</div>
          <div class="wk-body">20X1 reported revenue CU67,000, surplus CU7,000, closing accumulated surplus CU27,000. 20X2 accounting records show revenue of CU60,000 (including the misplaced CU6,500) and expenses of CU86,500.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            20X1 is RESTATED: revenue from taxation rises to CU40,500 (from CU34,000), restated surplus <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">CU13,500</strong>. 20X2 revenue is corrected down to exclude the CU6,500 already recognized in 20X1, giving 20X2 surplus of <strong style="color:var(--color-text-info);">CU11,000</strong>. Note discloses: increase in 20X1 revenue/surplus/debtors/net assets-equity of CU6,500 each, with NO effect on 20X2.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is a correction of an ERROR (para. 47) – restated via comparatives, not a current-period revenue/expense line.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="card cw">
        <div class="ctitle cw">Example 2 – voluntary policy change with retrospective application</div>
        <div class="worked">
          <div class="wk-title">Change from capitalizing to expensing borrowing costs on a power station under construction</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Previously capitalized: CU2,600 in 20X1, CU5,200 in periods before 20X1. Management judges expensing is more transparent and consistent with industry practice.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Opening 20X1 accumulated surplus reduced by CU5,200 (pre-20X1 adjustment). 20X1 restated surplus: CU18,000 − CU2,600 interest = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">CU15,400</strong>. 20X2 surplus (before interest CU30,000, less CU3,000 interest) = <strong style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">CU27,000</strong>. Statement of Changes in NA/E shows the CU5,200 policy-change adjustment as a distinct line, separate from the surplus for the year.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">This is a VOLUNTARY policy change (para. 17(b)) applied retrospectively (para. 27) – disclosed per para. 34.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card ci span2">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Example 3 – prospective application when retrospective application is impracticable</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;">
          <div class="wk-title">Adopting a fuller components approach and a current value model for PP&amp;E</div>
          <div class="wk-body">Existing asset records weren't detailed enough to apply a component approach fully in prior years. An engineering survey at the END of 20X1 provided current operational values, useful lives, and residual values for components going forward – but not enough to reliably reconstruct historical COST data for those components.</div>
          <div class="wk-answer">
            Management concludes it's impracticable to apply the change retrospectively, or prospectively from any earlier date. Applied PROSPECTIVELY from the start of 20X2: carrying amount of PP&amp;E increases by CU6,000 at the start of the year, a revaluation reserve of CU6,000 is created, and depreciation expense increases by CU500 for 20X2. No effect on any prior period.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement / Process Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">Decision tree – is it a policy change, an estimate change, or an error?</div>
        <ol class="ordered-list">
        <li>Did the item arise from NEW information or new developments about the future? &rarr; CHANGE IN ESTIMATE (prospective)</li>
        <li>Did the entity switch measurement basis, recognition, or treatment WITHIN or BETWEEN accounting bases? &rarr; CHANGE IN POLICY (retrospective, subject to impracticability)</li>
        <li>Was information available and obtainable at the time, but omitted or misapplied? &rarr; PRIOR PERIOD ERROR (retrospective restatement, subject to impracticability)</li>
        <li>Hard to tell whether it's a policy change or an estimate change? &rarr; treat as a CHANGE IN ESTIMATE (para. 40)</li>
      </ol>
        <div class="callout warn callout--flush">All three categories are corrected/applied WITHOUT hindsight – the impracticability test in §8 governs how far back a policy change or error correction can practically reach.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-spaced">
      <div class="card cs span2">
        <div class="ctitle cs">Concept-to-statement mapping table</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>IPSAS 3 concept</span><span>Statement(s)</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Retrospective policy change</span><span><span class="badge bs">SOFP</span> <span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>Adjusts opening net assets/equity for the earliest period</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Prospective policy change (impracticable)</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>No prior-period effect; hits current and future periods</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Change in accounting estimate</span><span><span class="badge bp">SOFPerf</span></span><span>Always prospective – current period, or current + future</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Prior period error correction</span><span><span class="badge be">NA/E</span></span><span>Restates comparatives; excluded from current-period surplus/deficit</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr 1.3fr;"><span>Impending Standard not yet applied</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Known/estimable impact disclosure</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2">
        <div class="ctitle cd">How IPSAS 3 connects across the IPSAS body of literature</div>
        <div class="td-table">
          <div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 1</span><span>General accounting-policy disclosure sits here; IPSAS 3 governs disclosure of CHANGES specifically (§5)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 5</span><span>Borrowing Costs – Example 2 retrospective capitalisation→expensing of borrowing costs (§11)</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 19</span><span>Contingency outcomes are estimate changes, not error corrections (§7)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 31</span><span>Investment property revaluation – Example 1 error scenario (§11); treatment of revaluation surplus</span></div>
          <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 33</span><span>First-time adoption of accrual-basis IPSAS – effective-date interaction (§9)</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.7fr 2.2fr;"><span>IPSAS 46</span><span>Measurement estimation-uncertainty guidance feeding para. 57 (§9)</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Retrospective application / restatement</div><div class="glossary-def">Applying a previously applied accounting policy to a prior period, or as if a prior period error had never occurred.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Impracticable</div><div class="glossary-def">Cannot be applied after making every reasonable effort; includes cases requiring hindsight-free assumptions about management intent or period-specific effects that are not determinable.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Prior period error</div><div class="glossary-def">A misstatement in prior-period financial statements from an omission, misapplication of an accounting policy, or fraud – corrected retrospectively.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Change in accounting estimate</div><div class="glossary-def">An adjustment to the carrying amount of an asset/liability from reassessing present circumstances and future benefits/obligations – recognized prospectively.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Measurement basis</div><div class="glossary-def">The different methods used to determine the amounts at which assets, liabilities, and equity are carried – e.g. historical cost, current cost, realizable value, exit value, current operating value, fair value.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Faithful representation</div><div class="glossary-def">A qualitative characteristic that requires representations in financial statements to be complete, neutral, and free from error.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

